                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I'm liking it very much.\n\nIt's a neat cover shooter and the movement and mechanics are done quite well. The control scheme is streamlined and makes sense. \"Wall stickiness\" is just right -- you don't feel like a magnet like in ME2 and ME3. Gunplay feels crunchy; shooting over-the-top from cover with the shotgun is a hoot, against near enemies. Dodge-roll feels weighty, as it should. The sprint has a touch of Gears \"roadie run\" about it but it's far easier to control. Graphics look darn good given the original release year.\n\nI like third-person shooters so this is scratching an itch quite nicely.\n\nAlso, it's quite simple to adjust the FOV. You can do it in-game via console command (not recommended), or by changing an INI file.I do not recommend doing it via console command, because increasing the FOV that way also reduces the iron sight zoom considerably. However, if you want this, while in-game, press TAB to open the console, enter fov 90 or whatever number you want, hit enter.\n\nThe preferred method is to open ScourgeCamera.ini from the Documents directory where saves and config files are kept: typically it's %USERPROFILE%\\Documents\\My Games\\Scourge Outbreak\\ScourgeGame\\Config\\ \n\nOpen ScourgeCamera.ini, and Under the heading [ScourgeGame.ScCameraMode_ThirdPerson] find the line FOVAngle=80 and change it to whatever you want (I use 90). There are also numerous other FOV adjustments in that file (for iron sights and the like), but I found simply changing that one value provided consistent, harmonious results across-the-board. That being said, I increased the two iron sight values from 50 to 60 (there's a setting for iron sight and another setting for in-cover iron sight; default for both of these is 50 and I changed to 60 -- so I have the main third-person FOV at 90, and the two iron sight FOV settings at 60, and it's perfect for my tastes; I left everything else unchanged and there are no jarring transitions at all whether you're sprinting or crouching or in-cover).",
